  "notes": [
    {
      "text": "Producer/enqueue (unknown impact): decided no code change is needed. enqueue() still resolves once the job is pending in memory; under the new contract that resolution is the point of acceptance, after which the job is delivered at least once while the queue process runs. It is not a durability acknowledgement: a queue process restart loses the job. The README states this. enqueue now also initializes attempts=0 and no lease, which is internal.",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    },
    {
      "text": "dequeue() was removed rather than kept: any destructive dispatch path would reintroduce the job loss this change removes.",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    },
    {
      "text": "Failed handlers are retried only via lease expiry (no explicit nack/release), so a failing job waits out its full lease before redelivery. A deliberate simplification, not a correctness gap.",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    },
    {
      "text": "ack() also rejects a lease that has expired by the clock but has not been reaped yet (reason lease_expired), and claim() reaps expired leases before choosing a job, so the queue does not depend on an external reaper.",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    },
    {
      "text": "Dead-lettered jobs are kept in memory and listed by deadLetters()/inspect(); there is no API to requeue or purge them. Default maxAttempts is 5 (configurable).",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    },
    {
      "text": "Out of scope and not done: exactly-once processing (EQ-C4 demonstrates the duplicate), durable storage, and process-restart recovery.",
      "source": "agent-reported"
    }
  ]
}
